(
  'master-subject-verb-agreement',
  'Master Subject-Verb Agreement: A Complete Guide',
  'Subject-verb agreement is one of the most common grammar mistakes. Learn the rules with clear examples and practice exercises.',
  E'# Master Subject-Verb Agreement: A Complete Guide\n\nSubject-verb agreement is a fundamental grammar rule that many English learners struggle with. This guide will help you master it once and for all.\n\n## What is Subject-Verb Agreement?\n\nSubject-verb agreement means that the subject and verb in a sentence must match in number (singular or plural).\n\n### Basic Rules\n\n**Singular subjects take singular verbs:**\n- ✅ She **writes** every day.\n- ❌ She write every day.\n\n**Plural subjects take plural verbs:**\n- ✅ They **write** every day.\n- ❌ They writes every day.\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n### 1. Subjects separated from verbs\n\nWhen words come between the subject and verb, students often make mistakes:\n\n❌ The bouquet of roses **are** beautiful.\n✅ The bouquet of roses **is** beautiful.\n\n*Tip: "Bouquet" is singular, not "roses"*\n\n### 2. Compound subjects with "and"\n\nTwo subjects joined by "and" are usually plural:\n\n✅ Tom and Jerry **are** friends.\n❌ Tom and Jerry **is** friends.\n\n### 3. Indefinite pronouns\n\nWords like "everyone," "someone," and "nobody" are singular:\n\n✅ Everyone **wants** to succeed.\n❌ Everyone want to succeed.\n\n## Practice with Wrytt\n\nThe best way to master subject-verb agreement is through practice. Submit your essays to Wrytt and get:\n\n- Instant detection of agreement errors\n- Personalized quizzes based on YOUR mistakes\n- Detailed explanations for each error\n\n[Start practicing now →](/analyze)\n\n## Advanced Rules\n\n### Collective nouns\n\nCollective nouns can be singular or plural depending on context:\n\n- The team **is** winning. (team as one unit)\n- The team **are** arguing among themselves. (individual members)\n\n### "Either...or" and "Neither...nor"\n\nThe verb agrees with the noun closest to it:\n\n✅ Neither the students nor the teacher **was** ready.\n✅ Neither the teacher nor the students **were** ready.\n\n## Quiz Yourself\n\nWhich sentence is correct?\n\n1. The group of students (is/are) studying.\n2. Either the cats or the dog (need/needs) food.\n3. Everyone in the classes (want/wants) good grades.\n\n**Answers:** 1. is, 2. needs, 3. wants\n\n## Conclusion\n\nMastering subject-verb agreement takes practice. Use Wrytt to:\n\n- Analyze your writing automatically\n- Get quizzes from your actual errors\n- Track your improvement over time\n\n**Ready to improve?** [Try Wrytt free today →](/analyze)',
  'grammar',
  8,
  true
),
(
  '10-common-grammar-mistakes-esl',
  '10 Common Grammar Mistakes ESL Learners Make',
  'Avoid these frequent errors that hold back ESL learners. Learn the correct usage with examples from real student essays.',
  E'# 10 Common Grammar Mistakes ESL Learners Make\n\nAs an ESL learner, you''re not alone in making grammar mistakes. Here are the 10 most common errors we see in student essays—and how to fix them.\n\n## 1. Article Errors (a/an/the)\n\n❌ I want to be doctor.\n✅ I want to be **a** doctor.\n\nRule: Use "a/an" for non-specific nouns, "the" for specific ones.\n\n## 2. Subject-Verb Agreement\n\n❌ She don''t like coffee.\n✅ She **doesn''t** like coffee.\n\n## 3. Preposition Mistakes\n\n❌ I am good in English.\n✅ I am good **at** English.\n\n## 4. Verb Tense Consistency\n\n❌ Yesterday I go to the store and bought milk.\n✅ Yesterday I **went** to the store and bought milk.\n\n## 5. Plural vs. Possessive\n\n❌ The student''s are studying.\n✅ The **students** are studying.\n\n## 6. Double Negatives\n\n❌ I don''t know nothing.\n✅ I don''t know **anything**.\n\n## 7. Word Order in Questions\n\n❌ Where you are going?\n✅ Where **are you** going?\n\n## 8. Using "Much" vs. "Many"\n\n❌ I don''t have much friends.\n✅ I don''t have **many** friends.\n\n## 9. Sentence Fragments\n\n❌ Because I was tired.\n✅ I went to bed early **because I was tired**.\n\n## 10. Confusing "It''s" and "Its"\n\n❌ The dog wagged it''s tail.\n✅ The dog wagged **its** tail.\n\n## How to Fix These Mistakes\n\nThe best way to improve is through **personalized practice**. That''s where Wrytt helps:\n\n1. Submit your essay\n2. AI finds YOUR specific errors\n3. Get quizzes targeting what YOU need\n\n[Start learning from your mistakes →](/analyze)\n\n## Why Generic Exercises Don''t Work\n\nMost grammar books give the same exercises to everyone. But:\n\n- You might be great at articles but struggle with tenses\n- Your classmate has the opposite problem\n- Generic practice wastes time on things you already know\n\n**Wrytt creates personalized quizzes from YOUR actual writing errors.**\n\n## Conclusion\n\nEveryone makes mistakes—that''s how we learn! The key is to:\n\n✅ Identify your specific weak points\n✅ Practice those areas intensively  \n✅ Track your improvement\n\nReady to stop making the same mistakes? [Try Wrytt now →](/analyze)',
